  • Got it — I treated the product.json comments as instructions and built the import commands accordingly.

  I added four separate Artisan commands so you can run them independently (no “all‑at‑once” importer):

  What I added

  - import:production-items → creates Items + Stocks from ingredient lists
  - import:production-products → creates Products from produced_product
  - import:production-recipes → creates Recipes + Recipe Ingredients (normalized per 1 unit of output)
  - import:production-records → creates Shifts, DailyProduces, ProductionRecords

  Each command supports --dry-run, --mode=merge|replace, and mapping options.

  Files added

  - app/Console/Commands/ImportProductionItems.php
  - app/Console/Commands/ImportProductionProducts.php
  - app/Console/Commands/ImportProductionRecipes.php
  - app/Console/Commands/ImportProductionRecords.php
  - app/Console/Commands/Concerns/ParsesProductionJson.php

  Default JSON path

  - real_data/receips-sweetooth-main

  ———

  ## How To Run (recommended order)

  php artisan import:production-items
  php artisan import:production-products
  php artisan import:production-recipes
  php artisan import:production-records

  ### With explicit branch + dry-run

  php artisan import:production-items --branch-id=YOUR_BRANCH_UUID --dry-run

  ———

  ## Department / Product Type Mapping

  These commands need departments and product types by location (HOT KITCHEN, PASTRY, etc).
  You can pass a JSON map (file path or inline JSON).

  Example file: department_map.json

  {
    "HOT KITCHEN": 1,
    "PASTRY": 2,
    "GELATO": 3,
    "CORNER STORE": 4,
    "TILL": 5,
    "CONCESSION": 6
  }

  Example file: product_type_map.json

  {
    "HOT KITCHEN": 3,
    "PASTRY": 4,
    "GELATO": 5,
    "CORNER STORE": 6,
    "TILL": 7,
    "CONCESSION": 8
  }

  Then run:

  php artisan import:production-products --department-map=department_map.json --product-type-map=product_type_map.json
  php artisan import:production-recipes --department-map=department_map.json --product-type-map=product_type_map.json
  php artisan import:production-records --department-map=department_map.json

  If you want the importer to auto-create missing departments/product types:

  php artisan import:production-products --create-missing-departments --create-missing-product-types

  ———

  ## Replace vs Merge

  All commands default to merge.
  If you want to wipe related tables first:

  php artisan import:production-recipes --mode=replace --force

  ———

  ## Key Import Logic (aligned to product.json instructions)

  - Products: produced_product becomes Product name (ID in parentheses becomes SKU).
  - Items: Ingredient names become Items (ID in parentheses becomes SKU).
  - Recipes: Ingredients become Recipe Ingredients linked to Product’s recipe.
  - Production Records: reference_no used as batch_number, production_date + table_info.date_time used for shift/record time.

  I also normalize recipes per 1 unit of output:

  ingredient_ratio = input_qty / quantity_produced

  If you want per-batch recipes instead, say so and I’ll flip the logic.

  ———

  ## Unit Mapping I Applied

  From the JSON data:

  - G → g
  - KG → kg
  - ML → ml
  - CL → cl
  - PCS → pcs
  - PORTION, PK, RO, SPN → unit
  - CU, CP → cup
  - SC → scoop

  If you want different mapping (e.g., CU → cup_serving), I can adjust.

